     <h1>🌽 Grains &amp; Concentrated Feed</h1>
    <p>While hay and grass form the base of a horse's diet, some horses need extra energy from grains — especially those that work hard, grow fast, or have special health needs.</p>

    <h2>🤔 Does My Horse Need Grain?</h2>
    <p>Horses that might benefit from grain:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Sport and competition horses with high energy demands</li>
        <li>Young growing horses (foals and yearlings)</li>
        <li>Pregnant or lactating mares</li>
        <li>Horses that are underweight</li>
        <li>Very old horses with dental problems</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A resting horse on good pasture usually does NOT need grain.</p>

    <h2>🌾 Common Grains Used for Horses</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Oats:</strong> The most traditional grain. Easy to digest, safe to feed.</li>
        <li><strong>Barley:</strong> More energy-dense than oats. Should be rolled or cooked.</li>
        <li><strong>Corn:</strong> Very high in energy — easy to overfeed, use sparingly.</li>
        <li><strong>Wheat bran:</strong> Often used as a mash. High in phosphorus — don't overfeed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🏪 Commercial Horse Feeds</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Pellets:</strong> Consistent nutrition, low waste</li>
        <li><strong>Textured/sweet feed:</strong> Mix of grains and molasses — horses love the taste</li>
        <li><strong>Senior feed:</strong> Specially formulated for older horses</li>
        <li><strong>Performance feed:</strong> High energy for competing horses</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>⚠️ Grain Safety Rules</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Never feed more than 2–3 kg of grain per meal</li>
        <li>Introduce new feeds gradually over 1–2 weeks</li>
        <li>Never feed grain to a hot, tired horse</li>
        <li>Store grain in airtight, rodent-proof containers</li>
        <li>Too much grain can cause laminitis, colic, or metabolic issues</li>
    </ul>
